{"kind":"report","text":"Documentation-set and MCP-adoption advice for request #107. Reviewed the uncommitted README.md, docs/ (all eleven files), and both example READMEs in the working tree on main.\n\nDOCUMENTATION VERDICT: the conditions are met, with one substantive correction required and three smaller issues. The not-a-catalog framing is pervasive and consistent — README (\"examples, not a closed catalog\"), docs/README (\"not a catalog or preferred limit\"), harness overview (\"starting kit, not a fixed application catalog\"), every per-harness page, and the Pi page all say it, and each points at the authoring guide. Custom creation, substitution, extension, and installation are fully explicit: authoring.md walks a complete minimal Tailapp through both the CLI and MCP lifecycles, and the absence of a directory-import command (--bundle names only built-in source sets) is stated honestly in three places. The Pi page's third-party-extension caveats and the OpenCode adapter-contract page are exactly the right posture.\n\nD1 (required): README Boundaries claims Tailapp source cannot use \"wildcards, multiplication, or generated ranges\". The compiler rejects unquoted asterisks (wildcards and multiplication) but ADMITS the JSONata range operator, merely bounding each range at 4,096 — and the nested-range allocation path flagged in my milestone-A determination ([1..4096].[1..4096], ~16.7M elements with no asterisk) therefore remains open in the approved v1 profile. Either make the sentence true by excluding the range operator (the bundled apps do not use it, and this matches the multiplication decision and its in-code rationale), or correct the sentence to \"bounded ranges\". I recommend exclusion: it closes the last admitted generative-blowup surface until the planned evaluator fork lands, and it makes the published security claim accurate. Note this is a code change with a RuntimeID bump, not just a doc edit.\n\nD2: session-cost's README honestly documents that Claude Code's cost field is named cost_usd_micros while the bundled normalizer expects cost_microusd, \"so native cost is recorded as zero until that field is mapped\". Documenting a known-broken default undercuts the immediate-value story when the fix is a one-line alternative lookup in the normalizer; map both names before publication and keep the doc note only for the Codex token-placement gap, which genuinely needs fixture evidence.\n\nD3: verify the OTEL_LOG_TOOL_DETAILS environment variable name against the current Claude Code monitoring documentation before publication; the content-gate variables have shifted names between releases and this page will be copy-pasted verbatim by users.\n\nD4 (minor): mcp.md's \"stable failure classes\" section admits MCP returns error messages without a structured code field. Several messages already embed their code as a prefix (query_budget_exceeded:, frontier_changed:); make that prefix convention uniform for all classified errors so agents can parse codes today, and note it in the doc.\n\nMCP 2026-07-28 ASSESSMENT (independent, researched against the published specification changelog at modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/changelog and the MCP blog release posts):\n\nWhat changed: 2026-07-28 makes MCP stateless. The initialize/notifications/initialized handshake is removed; every request carries protocol version and client capabilities in _meta; servers MUST implement a new server/discover RPC advertising supported versions (the spec names it explicitly as the backward-compatibility probe on stdio); ping and logging/setLevel are removed; all results carry a required resultType field; list results carry ttlMs/cacheScope cache hints; tools/list ordering should be deterministic; server-initiated requests are replaced by the multi-round-trip pattern; and Roots, Sampling, and Logging are deprecated with a twelve-month window.\n\nShould Tailapp adopt it? Yes — additively, in a v1.x minor release, and not as a precondition for publishing these docs. Tailapp's MCP surface is unusually well positioned: stdio-only, tools-only, no sessions, and none of the deprecated features (no roots, sampling, or logging), so nearly the whole adapter sits in the intersection of both protocol eras. The delta is small and local to the 121-line adapter: implement server/discover; accept per-request _meta protocol/capability fields and echo serverInfo in result _meta; add resultType \"complete\"; add ttlMs/cacheScope to tools/list (the tool list is fixed per binary — a long TTL and private scope are natural); ordering is already deterministic. The JSON-RPC error-code renumbering does not touch the codes Tailapp uses.\n\nCompatibility cost: the revision is roughly one month old. The official SDKs speak it, but the three harness clients Tailapp exists to serve talk the handshake era today, and dropping initialize would break the exact audience the immediate-value criterion names. The eras are cleanly discriminable on stdio — a legacy client's first message is initialize; a new client opens with server/discover or a _meta-tagged request — so dual dispatch is cheap and unambiguous. One caution: verify current harness clients tolerate unknown result fields before adding resultType and cache hints unconditionally; if any strict client rejects them, gate the new fields on the detected era.\n\nIs dual-era the right boundary? Yes. Support exactly two eras: the 2025-06-18 handshake era the harnesses speak now, and 2026-07-28 stateless. Do not chase 2025-11-25-only intermediates (tasks, elicitation-complete) — Tailapp uses none of them, and both were reshaped or removed in 2026-07-28 anyway. Sunset the handshake era only when Claude Code, Codex, and OpenCode all ship stateless clients, under cover of the spec's own twelve-month deprecation policy. Record in the MCP reference that the protocol era is adapter surface only: it never enters the runtime profile or revision identity, so adopting 2026-07-28 cannot invalidate any active Tailapp. One optional future tie-in worth a line in the doc: 2026-07-28 documents OpenTelemetry trace-context propagation conventions in _meta, which a telemetry product could meaningfully honor later.\n\nSummary: fix D1 (preferably by excluding the range operator, with its RuntimeID bump), apply D2, verify D3, consider D4, then publish the documentation set; plan dual-era MCP adoption as a small follow-up release."}